description:
Problem: TypeScript SDK consumers need ergonomic, portable adapters between AsyncIterator-style consumers (for node/Deno servers and many existing codebases) and WHATWG ReadableStream/TransformStream used in browser APIs and fetch streaming. Existing adapters in the codebase are fragile: they lose cancellation signals, can't resume after transient network failures, and do not surface backpressure across runtimes.

Goals:
- Provide an adapter layer that reliably bridges AsyncIterator <-> ReadableStream and supports tee/clone semantics for fanout.
- Preserve cancellation and propagate it both directions (iterator.return() -> stream.cancel(), stream.cancel() -> iterator.return()).
- Add a resumable checkpoint token approach for idempotent retries: clients can opt into lightweight checkpointing so a transient error can resume from last confirmed token.
- Ensure ergonomic API for both simple usage (one-liner helpers) and advanced control (watermark/backpressure tuning, transform hooks).
- Work cross-platform: Node streams, WHATWG ReadableStream in browser, and Deno compatibility.

Proposed approach:
1) Implement a small runtime-agnostic bridge class (AdaptiveHandoff) that exposes two views: asAsyncIterator() and asReadableStream(). The implementation internally sequences packets with a monotonically increasing "seq" metadata and optional checkpoint tokens.
2) Cancellation flow: when iterator.return() is called, the bridge calls cancel() on the stream controller (if present) and triggers a user-supplied onCancel hook. Conversely, stream.cancel() triggers iterator.return().
3) Backpressure semantics: implement an explicit waterline promise approach. Producers can await bridge.lowWatermark() to avoid overwhelming downstream; the default policy uses a highWater/lowWater token count (configurable). For browsers where internal stream backpressure is available, prefer pipeTo() signals.
4) Resumable checkpoints: the bridge emits checkpoint metadata periodically (configurable interval or on logical sentence boundaries). On transient network error, the SDK runtime will attempt a retry using the last acknowledged checkpoint token to request a range-resume from the server (requires server support). The default server-side contract is optimistic and best-effort; SDK will degrade gracefully if resume is not supported.
5) Tee/fanout: support a tee() helper that clones the stream view by duplicating packets and maintaining per-branch checkpoints. This is intended for cases like both logging and consumption.

Trade-offs and considerations:
- Checkpointing increases metadata overhead in streams; we will keep tokens compact (UUID4-like short base62) and emit at coarse granularity.
- Strong guarantees require server support for idempotent range/resume semantics; we'll implement best-effort client-side logic and provide clear docs/feature flags.
- Complexity vs ergonomics: ship ergonomic one-call helpers (fromAsyncIterator, toReadableStream) with safe defaults; advanced API exposes knobs for tuning.

API examples (usage snippets):
1) Simple adapter (iterator -> ReadableStream):
const rs = AdaptiveHandoff.fromAsyncIterator(myAsyncGen(), { checkpointInterval: 5 });
await fetch('/ingest', { method: 'POST', body: rs });

2) ReadableStream -> AsyncIterator consumer with cancellation propagation:
const bridge = new AdaptiveHandoff();
const iterator = bridge.asAsyncIterator();
const readable = bridge.asReadableStream();
// pipe a fetch response body into the bridge (browser or node fetch polyfill)
response.body.pipeTo(readable.getWriter());
// consumer can consume and early-return
for await (const chunk of iterator) {
  if (shouldStop(chunk)) { await iterator.return(); break; }
}

3) Resume on transient error (pseudocode):
try {
  await sdk.callStreamingEndpoint({ body: bridge.asReadableStream(), resumeToken: bridge.lastCheckpoint() });
} catch (err) {
  if (isTransient(err)) {
    const token = bridge.lastCheckpoint();
    // server supports resume token -> request resume
    await retryWithResume(token);
  } else throw err;
}
